/**
 * The acceptance instrument of the three-tier parity ExecPlan ({@code .agent/three-tier-parity-execplan.md}): one
 * benchmark method per {@link ThreeTierParityCorpus} cell, measured under each of the three execution regimes described
 * by {@link ThreeTierRegime}.
 *
 * <p>
 * The claim the plan has to prove is an ordering, per query: the adjacency regime is never slower than the LMDB-cursor
 * regime, and the Janino regime is never slower than either. Run this benchmark once per regime and feed the result
 * files to {@code scripts/three-tier-report.py}, which prints the matrix and flags any cell where a higher tier lost
 * beyond noise (an "inverted cell").
 *
 * <h2>How to run it</h2>
 *
 * A regime is a JVM-wide decision — compiled kernels and built adjacency planes outlive a system-property flip — so
 * each regime needs its own JVM. The cheap way to sweep many cells is one in-process run per regime, which builds each
 * dataset only once (see {@link ThreeTierParityFixtures}):
 *
 * <pre>
 * ./scripts/run-single-benchmark.sh --module core/sail/lmdb --class ThreeTierParityBenchmark \
 *     --method 'cycle3$' --param regime=lmdb &gt; benchmark-results/tier-m0-lmdb-r1-$(date +%F).txt
 * </pre>
 *
 * The theme cells ({@code theme*}) open the shared multi-gigabyte store that {@link ThemeQueryBenchmark} loads and
 * never rebuild it; run them separately from the generated-dataset cells, and load that store once with
 * {@code ThemeQueryBenchmark} first if it is missing.
 */
